<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 6: Where does Bias come from?  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--
  NOTES (see also notes/ch6-research.md):
  - Framework: Cathy O'Neil's three claims, as described in Woodson's review
    (2018): bad proxy variables, negative feedback loops, unsound statistical
    techniques. We have the review, not the book itself.
  - Case study: the SHA means test again (Chapter 4 told the story; this
    chapter looks at the machinery). All facts are re-used from Chapter 4,
    plus Lighthouse's electricity result.
  - Podcasts: 172 (proxy variables), 269 (David and Kate, why better data
    matters), 17 (how data lies; transcript only read through a summary).
  - Davies and Douglas is a draft chapter (17 Nov 2020). Only the central idea
    is used. We read about 40% of it closely.
  - TODO: Try It needs the village survey data set and the video link.
  - TODO: "AI world map: whose data is used?" (link needed).
  - Practise (STACK) section removed on purpose. Questions go in the exercises.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-where-does-bias-come-from" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Where does Bias come from?</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-where-does-bias-come-from">
        <p>
            Researchers working with farmers in West Africa want to know how
            much millet small plots have produced.
            They struggle to get reliable weights for the grain, so they weigh
            the seed heads at harvest instead.
            It turns out that this is a better measure.
            The grain can be eaten, lost or damaged before it's weighed.
            David tells the story in the IDEMS Podcast
            (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-172" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 172, 2025</xref>).
        </p>

        <p>
            Now think about a household in Nairobi that's asked what its roof
            is made of.
            The answer helps to decide what the household pays for health
            insurance.
        </p>

        <p>
            Both are <term>proxies</term>.
            They are something that's easy to measure, standing in for something
            that's hard to measure.
            The first one has been checked, and it works.
            The second one is what we looked at in Chapter 4.
            This chapter is about telling the difference.
        </p>

        <p>
            Bias can't always be removed from an AI system.
            But it can be recognised, reduced and managed, and that starts
            with knowing where it comes from.
            We look at three places.
        </p>

        <!-- Draft objectives: revise once the rest of the chapter is written. -->
        <objectives>
            <title>By the end of this chapter you should be able to</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Describe Cathy O'Neil's three-part framework for
                        recognising bias in AI.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Spot proxy variables, feedback loops and unsound
                        statistics in real cases.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain how missing, unbalanced or unrepresentative
                        data leads to harm.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use the framework to evaluate a real AI system, and
                        suggest improvements to its design or process.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 1. The framework                                               -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-where-does-bias-come-from-framework">
        <title>Three Ways Bias Gets In</title>

        <p>
            Cathy O'Neil is a mathematician who built models for finance
            companies and internet advertising firms before she wrote
            <em>Weapons of Math Destruction</em>.
            In the book she argues that harmful models, which she calls
            weapons of math destruction, tend to go wrong in three ways
            (<xref ref="biblio-woodson-2018" text="custom">Woodson, 2018</xref>).
            This is her framework, not ours.
            We use the description in Thomas Woodson's review of the book.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-where-does-bias-come-from-proxy">
            <title>Bad proxy variables</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A proxy is something easy to measure, which stands in for
                    something hard to measure.
                    It goes wrong when it isn't a good stand-in.
                    O'Neil's example is that models often judge a person as
                    risky or safe from where they live, who they know and
                    their credit score.
                    None of those measure whether the person is risky
                    (<xref ref="biblio-woodson-2018" text="custom">Woodson, 2018</xref>).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-where-does-bias-come-from-loop">
            <title>Negative feedback loops</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A loop is how a system finds out that it was wrong.
                    O'Neil's complaint is that analysts rarely update their
                    models by trial, error and correction
                    (<xref ref="biblio-woodson-2018" text="custom">Woodson, 2018</xref>).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-where-does-bias-come-from-stats">
            <title>Unsound statistical techniques</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Conclusions drawn from too little data, or from data that
                    doesn't represent the people it's used on.
                    O'Neil's example is that teachers are judged on the test
                    scores of 25 to 30 students.
                    Statisticians know you can't conclude much from a sample
                    that small
                    (<xref ref="biblio-woodson-2018" text="custom">Woodson, 2018</xref>).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            The phrase <q>feedback loop</q> is used for two different things,
            and it helps to keep them apart.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <em>A loop that corrects.</em>
                    In Chapter 2, the Post Office system got better because its
                    mistakes were caught and fed back.
                    The birdsong app had no such loop, and its mistakes stayed
                    hidden.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <em>A loop that reinforces.</em>
                    O'Neil's example is policing.
                    If police focus on certain crimes in one part of the
                    city, they find more crime there, which seems to justify
                    more policing, which finds more crime
                    (<xref ref="biblio-woodson-2018" text="custom">Woodson, 2018</xref>).
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            In Woodson's account, O'Neil's complaint about models that aren't
            updated is about the first kind, and her policing example is the
            second.
            Woodson asks in his review why some feedback loops improve a model
            while others trap people.
            That's a good question to keep in mind.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 2. Case study                                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-where-does-bias-come-from-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: Three Weak Points in One Score</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-recap">
            <title>Back to SHA</title>

            <p>
                In Chapter 4 we told the story of Kenya's health insurance
                means test.
                SHA uses 43 pieces of information about a household, like its
                roof, toilet and water supply, to estimate what the household
                spends, and the premium is 2.75% of that
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
                Here we look at how it's built, using O'Neil's three
                questions.
                Most of the facts are the same as in Chapter 4.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-sha-proxies">
            <title>The proxies</title>

            <p>
                SHA doesn't know what a household earns or spends.
                What it has is the answers to the questions.
                So everything in the test is a proxy.
                That's the same idea as in Chapter 1, where the loan apps look at
                behaviour on your phone.
                Neither the phone behaviour nor the roof is a direct measure of
                what the model wants to know.
                They are guesses from things you can see, which happen to
                correlate with it in the data the model learned from.
            </p>

            <p>
                Lighthouse looked at which households the proxies get wrong.
                The model has learnt a real link between electricity and
                wealth, because households with electricity are less often
                poor.
                But among households that are poor <em>and</em> have
                electricity, 56% were predicted not to be poor.
                Lighthouse found the same pattern for households that are mostly
                women, and for poor households where the head had finished
                secondary school.
                Poor households in richer counties were also misclassified more
                often than poor households in poorer ones
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                That's what a bad proxy looks like.
                It works on average.
                It fails for the people who don't fit the pattern, and those
                are poor households that happen to have a feature that's
                usually a sign of being better off.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-sha-data">
            <title>The data</title>

            <p>
                The weights in the formula were set using the 2021 Kenya
                Continuous Household Survey.
                The consultants who checked the model before launch, IDinsight,
                said that the survey was out of date.
                According to the reporters, they also said that it had too many
                middle-income households in it and very few data points from
                the poorest areas
                (<xref ref="biblio-au-2026" text="custom">Africa Uncensored, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                So the model knew the least about the households it was most
                likely to get wrong.
            </p>

            <p>
                There's a second weak point in the data.
                Lighthouse tested the model on answers from a household survey.
                The real form is filled in online, by households themselves.
                Lighthouse says that could change the results
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-sha-loop">
            <title>The loop</title>

            <p>
                Is there a way for SHA to find out that it's wrong?
            </p>

            <p>
                IDinsight recommended that people should be able to contest
                the prediction made about them
                (<xref ref="biblio-au-2026" text="custom">Africa Uncensored, 2026</xref>).
                When Florence Atieno appealed through an automated line, her
                appeal was turned down without a reason
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
                In March 2026, the High Court ordered a clear and accessible
                complaints mechanism
                (<xref ref="biblio-capital-2026" text="custom">Wakaya, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                And the model itself?
                In November 2025, SHA told Africa Uncensored that it was still
                using the original model.
                The reporters say it's unclear whether SHA adopted any of
                IDinsight's changes
                (<xref ref="biblio-au-2026" text="custom">Africa Uncensored, 2026</xref>).
                That's closer to the birdsong app than to the Post Office.
                Lighthouse also tested IDinsight's alternative design, and found it
                wasn't more accurate, so the problem isn't just about adopting one
                particular fix.
                It's about whether anyone is checking and learning
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                We haven't found evidence of a reinforcing loop in this case.
                The one that matters here is the loop that's missing.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-sha-table">
            <title>Putting it together</title>

            <table xml:id="tbl-where-does-bias-come-from-sha">
                <title>SHA's means test, through O'Neil's three questions</title>
                <tabular>
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>Question</cell>
                        <cell>What we see in SHA's means test</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Bad proxies?</cell>
                        <cell>43 answers about the home and household stand in for
                        what the household spends.
                        Poor households that have electricity, or whose head
                        finished secondary school, are misclassified more
                        often.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Unsound statistics?</cell>
                        <cell>The survey behind the model is out of date, and has
                        few of the poorest households in it.
                        The model's errors are largest at the extremes.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>A loop to correct it?</cell>
                        <cell>An automated appeal refused without a reason, and
                        no clear sign that the model has been updated.</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                The three don't sit side by side.
                They feed each other.
                A survey with few poor households makes the proxies fit them
                badly.
                And because nothing is fed back, the problem stays.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    When you look for where bias comes from, don't expect to
                    find just one place.
                    The weak points in a system tend to line up.
                </p>
            </insight>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 3. Podcast 172: proxies                                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-where-does-bias-come-from-proxies">
        <title>Proxies Done Right, and Wrong</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-where-does-bias-come-from-proxies">
            <p>
                This section comes from a conversation between me and David
                Stern about proxy variables, which I'd been thinking about
                after reading O'Neil's book
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-172" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 172, 2025</xref>).
                It's about 25 minutes long, and it's available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/172-proxy-variables/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-where-does-bias-come-from-podcast-172">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 172: Proxy Variables</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-where-does-bias-come-from-podcast-172"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/7940/172-proxy-variables.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-millet">
            <title>A proxy done right</title>

            <p>
                My definition of a proxy is that you can't observe something
                directly, so you use something correlated with it as a stand-in.
            </p>

            <p>
                David's favourite example is the millet.
                In some work in West Africa, researchers were struggling to get
                reliable weights for the grain from small plots.
                They found that weighing the seed heads at harvest was more
                reliable.
                You could do it on the spot, whereas the grain had to wait
                until it was taken off the heads, and in the meantime it might
                be eaten, lost or damaged.
                They checked it against data they trusted, and the correlation
                was extremely high.
                It was also cheaper.
            </p>

            <p>
                Even then, David adds a warning.
                It worked for that millet, in that study.
                He says that if you were breeding a new variety and you chose
                it for heavy heads, you could end up with a variety that has
                lots of head and no grain.
                What you're using the proxy for matters.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-schools">
            <title>Proxies for school quality</title>

            <p>
                Then we move on to a proxy that goes wrong.
                How would you compare schools?
                You can't measure how good an education is, so you might use
                grades.
            </p>

            <p>
                David points out that if schools are judged and funded on
                grades, a school has a reason not to take weak students.
                (He adds that he's being extreme and exaggerating.)
                And a school that takes in students with learning difficulties
                could be doing excellent work and still look bad on the
                indicator.
            </p>

            <p>
                He also tells a story from Tanzania.
                An indicator of school quality used in many projects was the
                ratio of staff to students.
                His account is that the country did very well at bringing the
                ratio down, by training more teachers, and that it was cheaper
                to train arts teachers than science teachers.
                They ended up with six times as many arts teachers as science
                teachers.
                Arts teachers taught about four hours a week, and science
                teachers about 24.
                Students saw that the arts looked easier, and there's now a
                shortage of scientists.
                That's David's account, and we haven't checked it.
                His summary is that it was a misinterpretation of an
                indicator.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-rankings">
            <title>When the proxy becomes the target</title>

            <p>
                My example from O'Neil's book is the US university rankings.
                She describes how the rankings choose their measures, and how
                universities then play the game.
                Fees weren't one of the measures, for example, so a university
                can charge what it likes.
                Administration staff know who the model wants, and applicants
                from certain backgrounds can get help with their CVs to match.
            </p>

            <p>
                David's point is the one to take away.
                If an indicator is a proxy for quality, and then everybody
                works towards the indicator, it can stop being a good proxy.
                People are no longer working hard at quality.
                They're working hard at the indicator, and the link between the
                two weakens.
                (People sometimes call this Goodhart's law.)
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-proxies-summary">
            <title>Summary</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A proxy can be better than the thing you wanted to
                        measure, but only if you've checked it.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What you're using the proxy for matters.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        When people aim at a proxy, it can stop being a good one.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Always ask <q>so what?</q>.
                        What is it about this variable that's connected to the
                        outcome?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 4. Why you cannot just delete the variable                     -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-where-does-bias-come-from-delete">
        <title>Why You Can't Just Delete the Variable</title>

        <p>
            In Chapter 4 we saw that COMPAS, the risk score used in US courts,
            doesn't ask about race
            (<xref ref="biblio-propublica-2016" text="custom">Angwin et al., 2016</xref>).
            ProPublica still found that it was wrong in different ways for
            Black and white defendants.
            How can that happen?
        </p>

        <p>
            Two academics, Benjamin Davies and Thomas Douglas, give an
            answer in a draft chapter from 2020.
            They say that you can leave race out of a model, but if race is
            related to the outcome in the data, a machine learning tool may
            learn a combination of other factors that does the same job.
            They call it a perfect proxy for race.
            If that happens, leaving race out does nothing to weaken the link
            between a person's race and the risk the tool gives them
            (<xref ref="biblio-davies-douglas-2020" text="custom">Davies and Douglas, 2020</xref>).
        </p>

        <p>
            Their paper is mainly about whether this is as wrong as using race
            directly, which is a hard question in ethics.
            They don't give a simple answer, and say that it depends on the
            reason you think discrimination is wrong.
            The part we need is the idea.
            Deleting a sensitive variable isn't enough, because other
            variables can carry the same information.
        </p>

        <p>
            That's why we say bias is managed, and not removed.
            You have to look at what the model does, as well as what it's given.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 5. Podcast 269: feedback and data quality                      -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-where-does-bias-come-from-feedback">
        <title>One Internet, and No Way Back</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-where-does-bias-come-from-feedback">
            <p>
                This section comes from a conversation between David and Kate
                Fleming, another director at IDEMS, about what makes data
                good
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-269" text="custom">Stern and Fleming, episode 269, 2026</xref>).
                It's part of a series that's loosely in conversation with Karen
                Hao's book,
                <em>Empire of AI</em>.
                It's about half an hour long, and it's available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/269-why-better-data-matters/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-where-does-bias-come-from-podcast-269">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 269: Why Better Data Matters</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-where-does-bias-come-from-podcast-269"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/12557/269-why-better-data-matters.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-one-internet">
            <title>We have but one internet</title>

            <p>
                Kate starts with a quote from Ilya Sutskever, a co-founder of
                OpenAI, in late 2024.
                Computers keep getting more powerful, she reads, but the data
                isn't growing, because we have but one internet.
            </p>

            <p>
                David's response is that the internet isn't high-quality
                data.
                To show it, he tells a story about something that happened to
                me.
                I was bouldering, and I hurt my ankle, and I wanted to know how
                high the wall was that I'd fallen from.
                An AI answer gave a height for the wall that was absurdly tall.
                (David retells it here.
                I tell the story myself in episode 125, which we use in Chapter 8, and
                there the summary said 22 metres.)
                That sounded much too high.
                I tracked it down, and the source was a post from the centre
                that was an April Fool's joke.
                The scraping of the internet couldn't tell it from a real
                announcement, and the AI picked it up.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-one-way">
            <title>A one-way street</title>

            <p>
                David's point is that if the centre had been able to go back
                and say that it was a joke, and to ignore it, that would have
                fixed it.
                There was no way to do that.
                He describes the process as one way: data is pulled out from
                wherever it's found, and nothing goes back to check it.
            </p>

            <p>
                Kate adds that humans are used to clean up some of this
                data, through what's called reinforcement learning from human
                feedback.
                But some data never gets flagged as needing a check, and is just
                accepted as fact.
                David says that there's a disassociation between the people who
                give feedback and the people who own the data.
                He says the process is a deliberate choice, built on
                extraction.
            </p>

            <p>
                And he says that once you've extracted data, you've lost the
                knowledge about its context that you'd have had if it had stayed
                with the people who own it.
                He gives Wikipedia as a better example, because it has a
                process for settling disagreements.
                It isn't perfect, he says, but it's more reliable than much of
                the internet.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-loop-types">
            <title>Back to the loops</title>

            <p>
                This is the first kind of loop from earlier in the chapter.
                The Post Office had a way for mistakes to come back.
                The birdsong app didn't.
                And the AI that told me about the wall didn't either.
            </p>

            <p>
                It's why a loop is a design choice.
                Someone has to decide who can say that something is wrong,
                and where that goes.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 6. Podcast 17: how data lies                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-where-does-bias-come-from-data-lies">
        <title>How Data Lies</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-where-does-bias-come-from-data-lies">
            <p>
                The third question is about statistics.
                This section comes from another conversation between me and
                David, in our responsible AI series
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-017" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 17, n.d.</xref>).
                It's about 24 minutes long, and it's available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/017-responsible-ai-how-data-lies/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-where-does-bias-come-from-podcast-017">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 17: Responsible AI, How Data Lies</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-where-does-bias-come-from-podcast-017"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/3086/017-responsible-ai-how-data-lies.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-simpson">
            <title>The same data, two stories</title>

            <p>
                David's example is Simpson's paradox, where a trend in the
                overall data reverses when you split it into groups.
                His example is digital literacy.
                Across a whole population, digital skills look like they fall
                with age.
                But within each age group, people's skills rise over time.
                Older people just start from a lower level.
            </p>

            <p>
                If you read only the overall trend, you'd conclude that older
                people are getting worse, and that training them is pointless.
                David says that would be wrong, and that training them is
                useful.
            </p>

            <p>
                His rule is that you shouldn't make <q>data-driven</q> decisions.
                You should make intelligent decisions that use data.
                The data has no answers, so you have to question it.
            </p>
        </subsection>





        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-data-lies-summary">
            <title>Summary</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The same data can tell two stories, depending on how you
                        group it.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        David wants intelligent decisions that use data, and
                        not decisions that are just driven by it.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The data has no answers by itself, so you have to
                        question it.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 6b. Too little data, and data that isn't there                 -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-where-does-bias-come-from-missing-data">
        <title>Too Little Data, and Data That Isn't There</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-where-does-bias-come-from-missing-data">
            <p>
                The episode is about reading data.
                O'Neil's third question is also about how much data there is,
                and whose it is.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-small-samples">
            <title>Too little data</title>

            <p>
                O'Neil's own example is how teachers are judged.
                A teacher may be assessed on the test scores of 25 or 30
                students, and a sample that small can't support a firm
                conclusion
                (<xref ref="biblio-woodson-2018" text="custom">Woodson, 2018</xref>).
                A teacher who has a difficult class in one year could look like a
                bad teacher.
                It's the same mistake as reading a lot into a small group.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-missing">
            <title>The data that isn't there</title>

            <p>
                There are two kinds of data that aren't there.
            </p>

            <definition xml:id="def-where-does-bias-come-from-known-unknowns">
                <title>Known unknowns</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Data that you know is missing.
                        An NA in a cell in a spreadsheet is one.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <definition xml:id="def-where-does-bias-come-from-unknown-unknowns">
                <title>Unknown unknowns</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Data that you don't know is missing.
                        These are harder, because nothing in the data tells
                        you that they're not there.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <p>
                The second kind is where bias often hides.
                In Chapter 5, David said that nobody has the image bank to
                train pest and disease apps on the plants and pests of the
                Sahel.
                An app built without that data would look fine to anyone who
                didn't know what was missing.
                SHA's survey, with few of the poorest households in it, is
                another example.
            </p>

            <!-- TODO: add the "AI world map: whose data is used?" visualisation
                 once we have the link. -->
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-where-does-bias-come-from-missing-summary">
            <title>Summary</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Small samples can't support big conclusions.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The data you know is missing is a problem.
                        The data you don't know is missing is a bigger one.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 7. Illustrative example                                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-where-does-bias-come-from-worked-example">
        <title>Try It: Is It the Village?</title>

        <p>
            Here's a small example of finding a proxy.
            A survey was done in four villages, to find out what affects the
            yield of rice.
            For each field, it recorded the village, the size of the field,
            the amount of fertiliser, and the variety of rice (Old, New or
            Traditional).
        </p>

        <p>
            When you compare the villages, two of them have lower yields than
            the other two.
            In an analysis of variance, the village accounts for a lot of the
            variation in yield.
            The variation between villages is large compared with what's
            left over, which is a mean square of about 464 against 111.
        </p>

        <p>
            But the first question to ask is <q>so what?</q>.
            What is it about a village that changes how much rice grows?
            Rainfall and soil type could be the answer, but they weren't
            measured.
            So maybe village is standing in for them.
        </p>

        <p>
            Fertiliser and variety were measured.
            When you put them into the model, the left-over variation drops to
            about 23, and adding the village makes no difference at all.
            The villages looked important because the farmers in some of them
            used higher-yielding varieties and more fertiliser.
            Village was a proxy.
        </p>

        <!-- TODO: add the data set and the steps to repeat the analysis in R,
             once the data and the video link have been supplied. -->

        <p>
            Think about what you'd have done if you'd stopped at the village.
            You might have sent help to the wrong place, or decided that the
            villages with low yields were just worse at farming.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 8. Exercises                                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-where-does-bias-come-from">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-where-does-bias-come-from-1">
            <title>Which of the three?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Choose a case from the course: SHA, the Tala loan app,
                    COMPAS, the Dutch childcare scandal, or the AI detectors.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Is there a proxy?
                            What is it standing in for, and who does it get
                            wrong?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Is there a loop?
                            Is it the kind that corrects, or the kind that
                            reinforces?
                            Is it there, or missing?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Is there a problem with the data or the statistics?
                            Is there something missing, too little of it, or
                            unrepresentative?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-where-does-bias-come-from-2">
            <title>Spot the proxy</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A government wants to help students from poorer families,
                    but it doesn't have full income data.
                    It decides to use the type of school a student goes to as a
                    stand-in for family income.
                    Public school students count as poorer.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Who would this get right most of the time?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who would it get wrong?
                            Think of a student at a public school and a student
                            at a low-cost private school.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What could you do to make it fairer?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-where-does-bias-come-from-3">
            <title>Ask <q>so what?</q></title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In the rice survey, the villages seemed to explain yield,
                    until fertiliser and variety were added.
                    Think of a pattern you've seen in data, or in the news,
                    where one thing seems to explain another.
                    What might it be standing in for?
                    Who could you ask to find out?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-where-does-bias-come-from-4">
            <title>When the target becomes the problem</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    David's Tanzania story is about aiming for a good
                    staff-student ratio and ending up with too few science
                    teachers.
                    Pick an indicator that's used where you live, for a school, a
                    clinic, a bank or a business.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What is it meant to stand in for?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            How could someone hit the target without
                            improving the real thing?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            How could you set it up so that this is harder?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-where-does-bias-come-from-5">
            <title>The loan app again</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In Chapter 1, the loan app looked at signals like how many
                    apps you have on your phone, and how thoroughly you fill in
                    the form.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What are those signals standing in for?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who might they get wrong?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What data would the app never see, and what
                            would that mean for people who were refused a
                            loan?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-where-does-bias-come-from-6">
            <title>A way back</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An AI tool says that a climbing centre has an
                    absurdly tall wall, because of an April Fool's post.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Who is the person who could correct it?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            How would they get the message to the people
                            who run the tool?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Would they be able to tell if it had worked?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What about someone with less power than a business,
                            whose false information is spreading?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-where-does-bias-come-from-7">
            <title>Known and unknown unknowns</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Think about data on health, farming or education in your
                    area.
                    Give an example of data that you know is missing.
                    Then give an example of data that might be missing that you
                    couldn't easily know about.
                    How would you find out?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-where-does-bias-come-from-8">
            <title>Too small a sample</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A teacher is judged on the test scores of 25 or 30
                    students.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What could make a good teacher's results look poor
                            in one year?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would you want to see before making a
                            decision about a teacher's job?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-where-does-bias-come-from-9">
            <title>Leaving it out</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    COMPAS doesn't ask about race, but its results still differ
                    by race.
                    In your own words, why isn't leaving a variable out enough?
                    Can you think of something in a Kenyan data set, like a
                    county or a language, that could stand in for something a
                    model isn't supposed to use?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-where-does-bias-come-from-10">
            <title>Evaluate a system</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Choose a real AI or data system that you know about.
                    Use O'Neil's three questions to evaluate it.
                    Then suggest one change to its design and one change to the
                    way people use it.
                    Which of the three problems would each change help with?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

